  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2 over x, z in [-2, 2], drawn as a surface at height 0.5 × loss with contours. It is 3 times steeper along x, so overshoot shows there first. Plain descent diverges along x once the learning rate is above 2/3.",
    "Each step = −learning rate × (true slope + noise). Noise is Gaussian with mean zero and spread 2·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch size b, so it is exactly zero at 64. A step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "Both arrows are drawn at their true step length (scale 1): orange is the mini-batch step the ball takes; grey is −learning rate × true slope. A hidden arrow means a step of 0.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or when the loss goes above 6 (diverged). The trail holds the start point plus one point per step. Steps play at 5 per second.",
    "The noise comes from a fixed seed, so Reset replays the same sequence. New run uses the next noise values and keeps the finished path as a faded ghost.",
    "Positions beyond the bowl's edge are drawn at the edge. The Loss readout always shows the true value."
  ]
}